# asn4-transcode-pooled-buffer -- RFC 6793 cross-context ASN4 transcode on the
# shared forward path (reactor/forward_body.go fwdUpdateForDestination)
#
# VALIDATES: RFC 6793 Section 4.2.2 -- when a route learned from a NEW (4-octet
# ASN) speaker is sent to an OLD speaker, AS_PATH is re-encoded to 2-octet form
# with AS_TRANS (23456) standing in for every non-mappable ASN, and the original
# 4-octet path is carried in a new AS4_PATH attribute (type 17).
# VALIDATES: RFC 6793 Section 4.2.2 second clause -- AS4_PATH is sent because the
# path is NOT composed of mappable four-octet AS numbers only. (wireu
# as4PathForPath owns that rule; hasNonMappableASN is the predicate.)
#
# PREVENTS: sending raw 4-octet AS_PATH bytes to a peer that never negotiated
# RFC 6793, which the peer reads as a different, corrupt AS path; and the
# converse, dropping AS4_PATH so the real 4-octet ASN is unrecoverable
# downstream.
#
# WHY THE RECEIVER IS iBGP -- this is the whole point of the file, and it is what
# makes it different from test/plugin/bgp-rs-asn4-transcode.ci:
#
# There are THREE transcode sites, and they are not interchangeable. Two of them
# are EBGP-only pre-passes that run BEFORE the shared body builder and hand it a
# wire whose context already says ASN4=false:
#   - reactor_api_forward.go:724 getEBGPWire()  (EBGP, non-RS-client: prepend + transcode)
#   - reactor_api_forward.go:732 / forward_rs.go:395  (EBGP RS-client: transcode, no prepend)
# bgp-rs-asn4-transcode.ci pins the second one -- its header says so, and its
# `rs-client true` is load-bearing for exactly that reason.
#
# The THIRD site is the one under test here: buildFwdBody sees srcCtxID !=
# destCtxID and calls fwdUpdateForDestination (forward_body.go:174), which takes
# a READ-POOL buffer (getReadBuf), runs wireu.TranscodeASPath into it, and
# re-parses zero-copy so the emitted sections ALIAS that pooled buffer. The
# buffer is written to TCP by a forward-pool worker long after the call returns,
# so the caller adopts the handle onto the ReceivedUpdate instead of releasing it
# (lifetime contract A, docs/architecture/memory/lifetime-contracts.md).
#
# An iBGP destination is what reaches it. facts.isEBGP is false, so NEITHER EBGP
# pre-pass runs, peerWire keeps the source (ASN4=true) context, and the ASN4
# mismatch is left for fwdUpdateForDestination to resolve. Make the receiver EBGP
# and this file silently re-tests one of the other two sites instead -- the same
# trap bgp-rs-asn4-transcode.ci fell into from the other direction.
#
# NO LOCAL_PREF in the source frame: the source is EBGP, RFC 4271 Section 5.1.5
# forbids it, and ze correctly tombstones it (RFC 7606 Section 7.5), which would
# change every byte offset asserted below.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=15s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# Map conn=N to a peer by remote IP instead of accept order (peer_connmap.go), and
# handshake both peers before any conn script runs -- otherwise which client
# connects first is a race and the announce can land on the receiver.
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip

# Source peer (conn=1, EBGP AS 65001, ASN4 negotiated): one UPDATE whose AS_PATH
# carries the non-mappable ASN 4200000000 (0xFA56EA00).
#   0000                withdrawn routes length = 0
#   0014                total path attribute length = 20
#   40010100            ORIGIN = IGP
#   4002060201FA56EA00  AS_PATH AS_SEQUENCE[4200000000], 4-octet (ASN4 on)
#   40030401010101      NEXT_HOP 1.1.1.1
#   180A0000            NLRI 10.0.0.0/24
# attrs = 4+9+7 = 20 = 0x14; body = 2+2+20+4 = 28; total = 19+28 = 47 = 0x2F.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF002F0200000014400101004002060201FA56EA0040030401010101180A0000

# The SOURCE's own EOR (RFC 4724 Section 4.1). Only conn=1's is asserted on the
# wire: the RFC constrains no ordering between the EOR and routes learned AFTER
# the initial update, so on conn=2 the forwarded route may legitimately arrive
# first, and pinning conn=2's EOR turns that permitted ordering into a flake.
# Same conclusion as bgp-rs-asn4-transcode.ci and bgp-rs-fastpath-ebgp-shared.ci.
# That BOTH EORs are sent is enforced by run_rs_observer(expected_peers=2), which
# fails closed when either is missing.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000

# Receiver peer (conn=2, iBGP AS 65000, ASN4 DISABLED): the transcoded UPDATE,
# byte-equality.
#
# Derived from RFC 6793 Section 4.2.2 for a NEW->OLD send with a non-mappable AS,
# and from wireu.TranscodeASPath's emission order (it rewrites attributes in
# place, in source order, then APPENDS AS4_PATH), NOT from observed output:
#   40010100            ORIGIN unchanged (default branch, copied verbatim)
#   40020402015BA0      AS_PATH re-encoded 2-octet: AS_SEQUENCE[AS_TRANS], and
#                       AS_TRANS = 23456 = 0x5BA0. Value length 6 -> 4, so the
#                       attribute shrinks by 2 bytes.
#   40030401010101      NEXT_HOP unchanged -- the destination is iBGP, so there
#                       is no next-hop-self and no AS_PATH prepend either: an
#                       AS_PATH of [65000, AS_TRANS] here would mean an EBGP
#                       pre-pass ran and this file is testing the wrong site.
#   C011060201FA56EA00  AS4_PATH (optional transitive 0xC0, type 17 = 0x11,
#                       length 6) carrying the original 4-octet 4200000000.
#                       RFC 6793 Section 4.2.2: it MUST be sent, because the path
#                       is not all-mappable.
#   180A0000            NLRI unchanged
# attrs = 4+7+7+9 = 27 = 0x1B; body = 2+2+27+4 = 35; total = 19+35 = 54 = 0x36.
# The length arithmetic is itself an assertion: TranscodeASPath backfills the
# attribute-length field, and an off-by-two there is exactly the bug that makes a
# peer read the trailing NLRI as an attribute header.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0036020000001B4001010040020402015BA040030401010101C011060201FA56EA00180A0000
EOF_PEER

# Observer: block until both peers are established and each has flushed its EOR,
# and until the forwarded 10.0.0.0/24 is on the wire, then shut down.
# Event-driven (subscribe + wait_for_event), never a sleep, and it fails closed
# on a stalled replay.

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	internal adj-rib-in {
		use bgp-adj-rib-in
	}
	external shutdown-after-up {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/asn4-transcode-pooled-buffer"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
			rs-fast-path enable
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process shutdown-after-up {
			receive [ update ]
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				asn4 disable
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
			rs-fast-path enable
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process shutdown-after-up {
			receive [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
# A transcode that could not get a buffer or failed to parse logs this and drops
# the route silently; the byte assertion above would then time out with no clue.
reject=stderr:pattern=encoding update for forward
